# Requirement acceptance and associated Verifier checks

The acceptance scope includes existing requirements and the original Verifier
checks associated with project rules and business experience actually adopted
during requirements, clarification, or planning. Recall records availability.
Adoption carries the corresponding Verifier into the same scope without a
separate candidate selection. At `constitution`, read project principles and
the published Birdview rule catalog. Experience retrieval and Verifier details
belong to later stages. Rules, knowledge, SOP, and architecture without an
original Verifier provide guidance. Retain the actual status if a Verifier
is missing, has no checks, or could not be read.

At `preview`, show both groups with their sources, original stages, pinned
versions, adoption decisions, and Verifier associations and status. Reuse
actual decisions for this scope. The engine can carry forward saved checks.
Initial recall happens in the earlier stages.
For an applicable [numeric precision rule](numeric-precision-domain.md), carry
its real requirement IDs and original checks as normal requirement items.
Record domain card review separately from those execution results.
Review the original definitions, determine applicability, and run the checks
at `finish/reverify`. Continue to use the existing authorization for stage
confirmation and upload.

## Pin and carry forward

Save and archive `FEATURE/acceptance-scope.json` through the normal stage
output contract. Pin it from `preview.json` with
`acceptance_scope: {path, sha256}`. Execution preparation validates the
reference and freezes it in `execution_context/frozen`. Stage approval uses
its own decision. Implementation and acceptance use the same list and
associate current results and evidence with individual items. Use the
existing revision flow for scope changes and retain history. Show a gap
when a legacy workflow lacks Verifier evidence.

Use `schema: "formsy.acceptance-scope.v1"`, the actual `project_id` and
`run_id`, `items`, and `coverage`. Each item has a unique stable `id`,
`assertion`, and `sources`. It may include `title`, `references`, or
`task_ids`.

- Requirement items use `kind=requirement` for all sources. Refer to the
  original requirements registered in the same run and stage and preserve
  their assertions. Use `methods` for the original local verification plan.
  For a newly enabled independent acceptance chain, include exact artifact
  references in each local program or manual method. A program method also
  needs its exact `argv` and, for secondary repositories, the absolute
  `worktree`. Match that command in Preview execution and cite its current
  native receipt when assessing the requirement.
- For a Verifier, include `verifier: {verifier_id, check_id, definition_source}`.
  The definition source has `kind`, `stage`, `path`, and `sha256`. It must
  come from the original saved `knowledge_read_verifier` result for this task
  and have a corresponding candidate, direct read, or adoption record.
  Require a ready `formsy.knowledge-verifier.v1` package, verifiable project
  and binding identity, and a check present in the original definition.
- Use the exact original `check.expected` for the Verifier assertion. The
  engine reads `expected`, `applies_when`, `criteria`, and `methods` from
  the definition. Keep an explicit read gap for a Verifier whose source
  cannot be verified.
- Include at least one source with the exact `kind`, original `stage`,
  `path`, and `sha256`. Valid kinds are `requirement`, `project_rule`,
  `knowledge`, `project_sop`, and `architecture`. Register source and
  definition references in their original stage of this run. Use
  `snapshot_ref: {path, sha256}` for the complete original snapshot registered
  in that stage. Validate both digests.

Set `coverage.status` to `complete` or `partial` with explicit `gaps`
and `sources` dispositions. Each source references its original snapshot
path and digest and has a real reason and one disposition from
`incorporated`, `guidance_only`, `not_applicable`, `superseded`, or `gap`.
Link incorporated sources to specific acceptance items. A complete read of
guidance with no Verifier may remain `guidance_only`. Retain the rationale
for actual experience adoption or exclusion. Evaluate each adopted Verifier's
applicability during acceptance. Mark failed or partial reads as gaps.

In `coverage.project_rules`, record the existing disposition for every
successfully recalled project rule. Include `worktree`, Server `project_id`,
the original `knowledge_ref`, `source: {path, sha256}`, `decision`, and
a concrete `reason`. Valid decisions are `adopted`, `not_applicable`,
and `reference_only`. A disposition for an aggregate source leaves individual
rule obligations in place. Carry all original Verifier checks for adopted
rules and preserve their identities, assertions, and definitions. Rules
without an original method remain guidance. Reuse the current adoption
decisions without adding a separate approval or selection. A gap in an
optional source leaves verified rule obligations in place.

Carry every declared FR and SC requirement ID into the same list. Keep
the exact spelling and leading zeros. Preview recognizes bold declarations
followed by a colon, plain or bold IDs in the first table column, and IDs
in explicitly labeled requirement or success-criteria traceability columns.
A traceability cell can list multiple IDs separated by a comma or slash.
Use the already registered specification bytes. A change to those bytes
uses the existing upstream revision checks and preserves the old record. For
example, retain the lint success condition for SC-007 in planning and
acceptance. The completion gate requires a current result for every
applicable original Verifier. A missing plan binding, an unexecuted check,
an unknown or failed result, stale evidence, or an invalid receipt blocks
completion. Verify current source and scope versions before using results.

Each list can contain up to 200 items and 1 MiB. Each item can have up to
20 sources and 20 methods. Reading observes only explicit references,
definitions, and snapshots. Recall, adoption, execution, and upload are
separate actions. Program receipts and document self-checks can support
acceptance evidence. Preserve the original acceptance items.

## Resume acceptance

At `finish/reverify`, use the same frozen list and retained receipts.
Check unexecuted, failed, unknown, invalid, stale, and actually affected
items. On first acceptance, assess every requirement that lacks current
evidence. Preserve valid unaffected judgments and previous failures.
Assess requirements individually with `flow acceptance-record` and run
original Verifiers through the local runner. Invalid requirement sources,
non-passing requirements, unresolved findings or tasks, and failing
Verifiers block completion. Acceptance uses actual execution evidence
and its existing scope. When assertions or evidence change, rerun
affected checks. Explicit independent audits retain their original
assertions and actual results.
